In pandas, string functions are not directly called on a DataFrame object. Instead, they are accessed through the .str accessor on a Series that contains string data (e.g., a DataFrame column with a string-like dtype).

This vectorized approach is a key feature of pandas, allowing you to apply string operations to every element in a Series without writing a for loop, making the operations fast and efficient.

How it Works
Select a String Column: You first need to select a column from your DataFrame that contains string data. For example, df['my_column'].

Use the .str Accessor: You then use the .str accessor on this Series: df['my_column'].str.

Call the Function: Finally, you can call one of the many string methods on the accessor, like df['my_column'].str.lower() or df['my_column'].str.contains('pattern').

Categories of String Functions
The functions are very similar to Python's built-in string methods. Here are some of the most common categories:

Case and Formatting
.str.lower(): Converts all strings to lowercase.

.str.upper(): Converts all strings to uppercase.

.str.title(): Converts the first letter of each word to uppercase.

.str.capitalize(): Converts the first letter of the entire string to uppercase.

.str.strip(): Removes leading and trailing whitespace.

.str.lstrip(): Removes leading whitespace.

.str.rstrip(): Removes trailing whitespace.

Splitting and Joining
.str.split(pat=None): Splits each string by a delimiter and returns a Series of lists.

.str.rsplit(): Splits from the right side.

.str.get(i): Extracts the element at index i from each list generated by a split.

.str.cat(): Concatenates strings in a Series with a specified separator.

Searching and Pattern Matching (Regular Expressions)
.str.contains(pat): Returns a boolean Series indicating if each string contains a pattern (e.g., a substring or regex).

.str.startswith(pat): Checks if each string starts with a given pattern.

.str.endswith(pat): Checks if each string ends with a given pattern.

.str.match(pat): Checks if the beginning of each string matches a pattern.

.str.findall(pat): Finds all occurrences of a pattern and returns a list of them for each string.

.str.count(pat): Counts the number of non-overlapping occurrences of a pattern in each string.

Replacement
.str.replace(pat, repl): Replaces a pattern with a new string.

String Properties
.str.len(): Returns the length of each string.

.str.isnumeric(): Checks if the string contains only numeric characters.

.str.isalpha(): Checks if the string contains only alphabetic characters.

.str.isdigit(): Checks if the string contains only digits.

.str.isspace(): Checks if the string contains only whitespace.

